.TH ESDM\-OPENSSL\-PROVIDER 7 "2026-08-05" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-openssl\-provider \- OpenSSL 3 providers backed by the ESDM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B libesdm\-rng\-provider.so
.B libesdm\-rng\-provider\-pr.so
.B libesdm\-seed\-src\-provider.so
.B libesdm\-egd\-provider.so
.B libesdm\-egd\-provider\-pr.so
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are OpenSSL 3 providers that answer OpenSSL's random number requests
from
.BR esdm\-server (8)
rather than from OpenSSL's own DRBG chain. They are built with the meson
option
.BR openssl\-rand\-provider .
.PP
Each provider offers the four RAND algorithms OpenSSL knows \-
.BR CTR\-DRBG ", " HASH\-DRBG ", " HMAC\-DRBG " and " SEED\-SRC
\- and routes all of them to the ESDM. Which one an application asks for
therefore no longer selects a construction; it selects nothing at all, because
the data comes from the ESDM either way. The algorithms are told apart from
OpenSSL's own by their property string, which is what an
.I openssl.cnf
selects on.
.SS The variants
.TP
.B libesdm\-rng\-provider.so
Property
.BR provider=esdm .
Talks the ESDM RPC protocol. Honors the prediction\-resistance flag OpenSSL
passes per request.
.TP
.B libesdm\-rng\-provider\-pr.so
Property
.BR provider=esdm .
The same, except that every generate and seed request goes to the
prediction\-resistance generator of the ESDM regardless of what OpenSSL asked
for. Its provider name reports "ESDM RNG Provider (PR)".
.TP
.B libesdm\-seed\-src\-provider.so
Property
.BR provider=esdm .
Offers
.B SEED\-SRC
only. For a configuration that wants OpenSSL to keep running its own DRBG but
to take its seed material from the ESDM.
.TP
.B libesdm\-egd\-provider.so
Property
.BR provider=esdm\-egd .
Talks the EGD protocol instead of the RPC protocol, and therefore needs
nothing but a single UNIX domain stream socket \- no protobuf, no shared
memory, no second socket. This is the variant for environments the RPC
interface cannot reach, a chroot that can be handed exactly one socket being
the usual case. See
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3)
for what the protocol can and cannot do.
.TP
.B libesdm\-egd\-provider\-pr.so
Property
.BR provider=esdm\-egd\-pr .
The EGD variant bound to the prediction\-resistance socket. The EGD protocol
cannot select the generator per request, so this is purely a matter of which
socket the provider connects to.
.PP
The distinct property strings mean several of these can be loaded side by side
and be selected individually.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Providers are activated in
.BR config (5),
i.e. in
.IR openssl.cnf .
Merely activating one is not enough to make OpenSSL use it: without a
.B random
section OpenSSL remains free to pick the default provider, so pin both the DRBG
and its seed source to the properties of the ESDM provider.
.PP
.RS
.nf
openssl_conf = openssl_init

[openssl_init]
providers = provider_sect
random = random_sect

[provider_sect]
esdm = esdm_sect
default = default_sect

[default_sect]
activate = 1

[esdm_sect]
activate = 1
module = /usr/lib64/libesdm\-rng\-provider.so

[random_sect]
random = CTR\-DRBG
properties = provider=esdm
seed = SEED\-SRC
seed_properties = provider=esdm
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B default
provider stays activated because the rest of OpenSSL needs its algorithms; only
the random section is redirected.
.SS EGD socket selection
The EGD providers take their socket path, in decreasing precedence, from the
.B egd_socket
key of their provider section in
.IR openssl.cnf ,
from the
.B ESDM_EGD_SOCKET
environment variable, and from the path the ESDM was built with. Configuring
the EGD variant therefore differs from the above only in the module and in the
properties:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[esdm_sect]
activate = 1
module = /usr/lib64/libesdm\-egd\-provider.so
egd_socket = /run/esdm\-egd.socket

[random_sect]
random = CTR\-DRBG
properties = provider=esdm\-egd
seed = SEED\-SRC
seed_properties = provider=esdm\-egd
.fi
.RE
.PP
The ESDM server serves that socket when started with
.B \-\-egd_socket
or through the shipped EGD socket units.
.SH NOTES
The reported security strength is 256 bits and the maximum request size is 256
bytes, which is the granularity OpenSSL's own DRBG chain uses as well; larger
consumer requests are split by OpenSSL.
.PP
A provider that cannot reach the ESDM fails the request rather than falling
back to another source \- otherwise pinning OpenSSL to the ESDM would be
advisory rather than binding.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ESDM_EGD_SOCKET
Path of the EGD socket, used by the EGD providers when
.I openssl.cnf
does not name one.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check that the provider is loaded and serving:
.PP
.RS
.nf
openssl list \-random\-generators \-provider esdm\-rng\-provider
openssl rand \-hex 32
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3),
.BR config (5),
.BR provider (7)
